{
 "id": "P17",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "More reasoning doesn't raise false alarms on clean code",
 "section": "results",
 "order": 35,
 "caption": "Share of the 500 clean programs each monitor scores 5/10 or higher, at each reasoning setting. Each step on the axis is one program. Every model stays between 0.2% and 0.8% at every setting, while P03 shows 6 of 8 catching more backdoors as effort rises.",
 "notes": [
  "At a fixed audit budget the false-positive rate is fixed by construction: 2% of clean code gets audited whatever the monitor. This asks a different question: does more reasoning push clean code's scores up? It doesn't; Haiku 5.5's mean clean score even falls as effort rises.",
  "Extends A06's false-alarm panel from two models to all eight."
 ],
